### What is Vert.x?

Vert.x is a toolkit for building reactive, event-driven, non-blocking applications on the JVM. It runs application code as lightweight units called verticles on a small pool of event-loop threads, following the reactor pattern. Each event loop handles many concurrent connections by never blocking, instead registering callbacks or futures that fire when I/O completes.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import io.vertx.core.AbstractVerticle;
import io.vertx.core.Vertx;

public class BalanceVerticle extends AbstractVerticle {
    @Override
    public void start() {
        vertx.createHttpServer()
                .requestHandler(req -> {
                    String accountId = req.getParam("id");
                    // Non-blocking: responds immediately
                    req.response()
                            .putHeader("Content-Type", "application/json")
                            .end("{\"account\":\"" + accountId
                                    + "\",\"balance\":150000.00}");
                })
                .listen(8080)
                .onSuccess(s -> System.out.println(
                        "National Bank of India API running on port 8080"));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vertx.vertx().deployVerticle(new BalanceVerticle());
    }
}
```

```text
National Bank of India API running on port 8080
```

### Why does the event-loop model work?

Because I/O is the bottleneck in most services, not CPU. A traditional thread-per-request model creates thousands of threads, each blocked waiting for a database or network response, wasting memory on stack space. The event loop handles all connections on one thread by never waiting. When an I/O operation starts, the thread moves on to the next event, and a callback fires when the I/O completes.

- A typical thread-per-request server needs 1,000 threads for 1,000 connections, each consuming about 1 MB of stack space.
- A Vert.x event loop handles 1,000 connections on one thread, using kilobytes of state per connection.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import io.vertx.core.AbstractVerticle;
import io.vertx.core.Future;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class NonBlockingLookup extends AbstractVerticle {
    @Override
    public void start() {
        vertx.createHttpServer()
                .requestHandler(req -> {
                    // Non-blocking database lookup
                    lookupBalance("SAV2001")
                            .onSuccess(balance -> req.response().end(
                                    "Rajesh's balance: " + balance))
                            .onFailure(err -> req.response()
                                    .setStatusCode(500).end(err.getMessage()));
                })
                .listen(8080);
    }

    private Future<BigDecimal> lookupBalance(String accountId) {
        // Simulates async database call
        return vertx.executeBlocking(promise -> {
            // This runs on the worker pool, NOT the event loop
            BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("150000.00");
            promise.complete(balance);
        });
    }
}
```

### How do verticles work?

Verticles are the deployment unit. A standard verticle runs on an event loop and must stay non-blocking. A worker verticle runs on the worker pool and may block. You deploy them with `vertx.deployVerticle()` and can scale by deploying multiple instances.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import io.vertx.core.AbstractVerticle;
import io.vertx.core.DeploymentOptions;
import io.vertx.core.Vertx;

public class DeploymentDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vertx vertx = Vertx.vertx();

        // Deploy a standard verticle (event loop)
        vertx.deployVerticle(new TransferVerticle());

        // Deploy a worker verticle (may block)
        vertx.deployVerticle(new StatementVerticle(),
                new DeploymentOptions().setWorker(true));

        // Deploy multiple instances for scale
        vertx.deployVerticle(TransferVerticle.class.getName(),
                new DeploymentOptions().setInstances(4));
    }
}
```

### How does the event bus work?

The event bus is the nervous system. You send point-to-point messages with `request`, broadcast with `publish`, and register consumers with `consumer`. In clustered mode the event bus spans JVMs, giving location transparency.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import io.vertx.core.AbstractVerticle;
import io.vertx.core.Vertx;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class EventBusDemo extends AbstractVerticle {
    @Override
    public void start() {
        // Consumer: handles balance requests
        vertx.eventBus().consumer("account.balance", msg -> {
            String accountId = (String) msg.body();
            BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("250000.00");
            msg.reply(balance.toString());
            System.out.println("Looked up balance for " + accountId);
        });

        // Producer: requests a balance
        vertx.eventBus().request("account.balance", "SAV2001", reply -> {
            if (reply.succeeded()) {
                System.out.println("Rajesh's balance: " + reply.result().body());
            }
        });
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vertx.vertx().deployVerticle(new EventBusDemo());
    }
}
```

```text
Looked up balance for SAV2001
Rajesh's balance: 250000.00
```

### What happens if you block the event loop?

Vert.x detects blocking and logs a warning. One blocking call stalls thousands of concurrent connections because the event loop cannot process any other events while blocked.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import io.vertx.core.AbstractVerticle;

public class BlockingMistake extends AbstractVerticle {
    @Override
    public void start() {
        vertx.createHttpServer()
                .requestHandler(req -> {
                    // WRONG: this blocks the event loop
                    try {
                        Thread.sleep(5000); // simulates slow JDBC
                    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    }
                    req.response().end("Done");
                })
                .listen(8080);
    }
}
```

```text
WARNING: Thread Thread[vert.x-eventloop-thread-0] has been blocked for 5000 ms
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import io.vertx.core.AbstractVerticle;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CorrectBlocking extends AbstractVerticle {
    @Override
    public void start() {
        vertx.createHttpServer()
                .requestHandler(req -> {
                    // CORRECT: move blocking work off the event loop
                    vertx.executeBlocking(promise -> {
                        BigDecimal balance = slowJdbcLookup("SAV2001");
                        promise.complete(balance);
                    }).onSuccess(balance ->
                            req.response().end("Balance: " + balance));
                })
                .listen(8080);
    }

    private BigDecimal slowJdbcLookup(String accountId) {
        // Simulates slow database call
        return new BigDecimal("150000.00");
    }
}
```

### What are the advantages of Vert.x?

The advantages are significant for I/O-bound microservices and gateways.

- Very high throughput with very few threads, because the event loop never blocks.
- Low memory per connection compared to thread-per-request servers.
- A modular, polyglot toolkit: use only the components you need, in Java, Kotlin, or JavaScript.
- A clean concurrency model that mostly avoids locking because each verticle is effectively single-threaded.
- Async composition with `Future` and `Promise`, or reactive streams integration with Mutiny and RxJava.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import io.vertx.core.Future;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FutureComposition {
    // Fluent async composition: no callbacks, no blocking
    public Future<BigDecimal> transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        return getAccount(from)
                .compose(fromAcc -> debit(fromAcc, amount))
                .compose(debited -> credit(to, amount))
                .onSuccess(v -> System.out.println(
                        "Transferred " + amount + " from " + from + " to " + to))
                .onFailure(err -> System.out.println(
                        "Transfer failed: " + err.getMessage()));
    }

    private Future<String> getAccount(String id) {
        return Future.succeededFuture(id);
    }

    private Future<BigDecimal> debit(String account, BigDecimal amount) {
        return Future.succeededFuture(amount);
    }

    private Future<BigDecimal> credit(String account, BigDecimal amount) {
        return Future.succeededFuture(amount);
    }
}
```

### What are the disadvantages of Vert.x?

The disadvantages are worth being honest about in an interview.

- A steeper mental model built on futures and callbacks instead of straight-line blocking code.
- The constant danger that one accidental blocking call stalls thousands of requests.
- Harder debugging because stack traces are fragmented across async boundaries.
- Fewer out-of-the-box conventions than a full framework like Spring Boot.
- CPU-heavy work still needs worker threads, so the event loop only helps I/O-bound workloads.

### How is Vert.x different from Spring WebFlux?

| Feature | Vert.x | Spring WebFlux |
|---|---|---|
| Foundation | Netty + custom event loop | Netty + Project Reactor |
| Unit of deployment | Verticle | Spring bean |
| Async primitive | Future/Promise | Mono/Flux |
| Communication | Event bus | Method calls / messaging |
| Opinionated | No: toolkit, pick what you need | Yes: Spring conventions |
| Clustering | Built-in clustered event bus | Requires external messaging |

```java
// Vert.x: event bus for inter-component communication
vertx.eventBus().request("account.balance", "SAV2001", reply -> {
    System.out.println("Balance: " + reply.result().body());
});

// Spring WebFlux: direct method call with Mono
accountService.getBalance("SAV2001")
        .subscribe(balance -> System.out.println("Balance: " + balance));
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Vert.x shines for I/O-bound, high-connection workloads like API gateways, real-time streaming services, and event-driven microservices. Pankaj at National Bank of India chose Vert.x for the high-traffic balance-inquiry API because it needed to handle 50,000 concurrent connections from mobile clients with minimal hardware.

- Monitor event-loop block time with the Vert.x metrics SPI.
- Size the worker pool deliberately based on the expected blocking workload.
- Use `vertx.createSharedWorkerExecutor("batch-pool", 10)` for dedicated pools.
- In production, deploy multiple verticle instances to use all CPU cores.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import io.vertx.core.Vertx;
import io.vertx.core.VertxOptions;

public class ProductionConfig {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        VertxOptions options = new VertxOptions()
                .setEventLoopPoolSize(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors() * 2)
                .setWorkerPoolSize(20)
                .setBlockedThreadCheckInterval(5000); // 5 seconds

        Vertx vertx = Vertx.vertx(options);
        System.out.println("Event loops: " + options.getEventLoopPoolSize());
        System.out.println("Worker pool: " + options.getWorkerPoolSize());
    }
}
```

```text
Event loops: 16
Worker pool: 20
```
